      "Key": "seduce",
      "Title": "Seduce, don\u0027t just observe",
      "Status": "warn",
      "Evidence": "The piece is dense with exact, seductive textures early \u2014 the ads that \u0027cut off dead at the median,\u0027 the car \u0027gone gray \u2014 the color a thing turns when nobody argues with it anymore,\u0027 the building read \u0027bottom to top\u0027 like a scar history, the CramIt bar tasting \u0027like chocolate described to someone who\u0027d never eaten food\u0027 \u2014 and these do pull the reader in with lived-in wrongness before horror arrives. But once the schism/mesh-intrusion plot takes over, the prose shifts almost entirely to procedural, bureaucratic rhythm (forms, clearances, timestamps, cost lines) and the sensory allure thins dramatically; the horror (the sound \u0027above his chair,\u0027 the locker, the thing in her mesh) lands mostly through clinical description and dialogue exposition rather than through the same seductive texture-work established at the opening.",
      "Fix": "Sustain the material, sensory specificity established in the opening (smells, light, texture of decay and mismatch) into the mid-and-late sections so the horror of the schism and the mesh-intrusion arrives wrapped in the same strange-beautiful concreteness rather than receding into procedural/administrative exposition."
    },
